### TITLE ###
NotInvertsRefinement
### DESCRIPTION ###
`if not (x == null):` should be equivalent to `if x != null:` -
truthy branch narrows x to non-null.
### INPUT ###
fn f(x: int?):
    if not (x == null):
        y = x
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  x @ 2:13 -> int?
  y @ 3:9 -> <no-type>
  x @ 3:13 -> int

# Symbol types
  f (fn): fn(int?) -> void
  x (param): int?
  y (local): int

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
AndChainsRefinements
### DESCRIPTION ###
`if x != null and y != null:` should narrow both x and y to non-null
in the body. The right operand is evaluated with the left's truthy
refinement active.
### INPUT ###
fn f(x: int?, y: str?):
    if x != null and y != null:
        a = x
        b = y
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  x @ 2:8 -> int?
  y @ 2:22 -> str?
  a @ 3:9 -> <no-type>
  x @ 3:13 -> int
  b @ 4:9 -> <no-type>
  y @ 4:13 -> str

# Symbol types
  a (local): int
  b (local): str
  f (fn): fn(int?, str?) -> void
  x (param): int?
  y (param): str?

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
AndShortCircuitRightSeesLeftNarrowed
### DESCRIPTION ###
In `x != null and type_of(x) == "int"`, the right hand sees x as
non-null (left's truthy refinement). With base type int?, the
right operand's narrowing to int is still useful (type_of can
further refine within the non-null component).
### INPUT ###
fn f(x: int?):
    if x != null and type_of(x) == "int":
        y = x
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  x @ 2:8 -> int?
  type_of @ 2:22 -> dynamic
  x @ 2:30 -> int
  y @ 3:9 -> <no-type>
  x @ 3:13 -> int

# Symbol types
  f (fn): fn(int?) -> void
  x (param): int?
  y (local): int

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
OrFalsyBothFalse
### DESCRIPTION ###
`if x == null or y == null:` truthy = at least one is null (no
refinement we can express). Falsy = both non-null - narrows both
in the else.
### INPUT ###
fn f(x: int?, y: str?):
    if x == null or y == null:
        a = 0
    else:
        b = x
        c = y
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  x @ 2:8 -> int?
  y @ 2:21 -> str?
  a @ 3:9 -> <no-type>
  b @ 5:9 -> <no-type>
  x @ 5:13 -> int
  c @ 6:9 -> <no-type>
  y @ 6:13 -> str

# Symbol types
  a (local): int
  b (local): int
  c (local): str
  f (fn): fn(int?, str?) -> void
  x (param): int?
  y (param): str?

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
NotAndDeMorgan
### DESCRIPTION ###
`not (x == null or y == null)` should give us "both non-null"
via the Negate path - flipping or's WhenFalse into the outer
WhenTrue.
### INPUT ###
fn f(x: int?, y: str?):
    if not (x == null or y == null):
        a = x
        b = y
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  x @ 2:13 -> int?
  y @ 2:26 -> str?
  a @ 3:9 -> <no-type>
  x @ 3:13 -> int
  b @ 4:9 -> <no-type>
  y @ 4:13 -> str

# Symbol types
  a (local): int
  b (local): str
  f (fn): fn(int?, str?) -> void
  x (param): int?
  y (param): str?

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
AndRightOperandSeesLeftNullGuard
### DESCRIPTION ###
The canonical defensive idiom. `and` only reaches its right operand
when the left was truthy, so `len(a)` sees a as str, not str?. Before
shortCircuitRight was shared with the synthesis pass this reported a
false positive on provably-safe code.
### INPUT ###
fn f(a: str?):
    if a != null and len(a) > 3:
        n = a
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  a @ 2:8 -> str?
  len @ 2:22 -> dynamic
  a @ 2:26 -> str
  n @ 3:9 -> <no-type>
  a @ 3:13 -> str

# Symbol types
  a (param): str?
  f (fn): fn(str?) -> void
  n (local): str

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
OrRightOperandSeesLeftNullCheckFalsy
### DESCRIPTION ###
Mirror of the `and` case: `or` reaches its right operand only when
the left was falsy, so `b == null` being false makes b a str there.
### INPUT ###
fn f(b: str?):
    if b == null or len(b) > 3:
        n = 0
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  b @ 2:8 -> str?
  len @ 2:21 -> dynamic
  b @ 2:25 -> str
  n @ 3:9 -> <no-type>

# Symbol types
  b (param): str?
  f (fn): fn(str?) -> void
  n (local): int

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
OrRightOperandSeesTypeOfFalsy
### DESCRIPTION ###
The falsy side of a type_of test narrows the right operand too. This
was the single biggest source of checker noise on real scripts - one
`or` chain in one script accounted for 9 of 11 remaining diagnostics.
### INPUT ###
fn f(c: str|int):
    if type_of(c) != "str" or len(c) > 3:
        n = 0
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  type_of @ 2:8 -> dynamic
  c @ 2:16 -> str|int
  len @ 2:31 -> dynamic
  c @ 2:35 -> str
  n @ 3:9 -> <no-type>

# Symbol types
  c (param): str|int
  f (fn): fn(str|int) -> void
  n (local): int

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
AndLeftOperandNotNarrowedByRight
### DESCRIPTION ###
Guard written second: the use genuinely isn't protected, because the
left operand is evaluated before anything is known. Must still report.
### INPUT ###
fn f(a: str?):
    if len(a) > 3 and a != null:
        n = 0
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  len @ 2:8 -> dynamic
  a @ 2:12 -> str?
  a @ 2:23 -> str?
  n @ 3:9 -> <no-type>

# Symbol types
  a (param): str?
  f (fn): fn(str?) -> void
  n (local): int

# Diagnostics
  [hint] RAD30001 @ 2:12 - Argument type 'str?' is not assignable to expected type 'str|list|map'

### TITLE ###
AndRightOperandSeesTruthySideOnly
### DESCRIPTION ###
Pins the direction of the refinement. `a == null` being TRUE is what
lets the right operand run, so there a is definitely null and len(a)
still reports. Without this case a sign flip in shortCircuitRight
would leave every positive case green.
### INPUT ###
fn f(a: str?):
    if a == null and len(a) > 3:
        n = 0
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  a @ 2:8 -> str?
  len @ 2:22 -> dynamic
  a @ 2:26 -> null
  n @ 3:9 -> <no-type>

# Symbol types
  a (param): str?
  f (fn): fn(str?) -> void
  n (local): int

# Diagnostics
  [error] RAD30001 @ 2:26 - Argument type 'null' is not assignable to expected type 'str|list|map'

### TITLE ###
OrRightOperandSeesFalsySideOnly
### DESCRIPTION ###
The `or` mirror of the sign-flip guard: `a != null` being FALSE is
what lets the right operand run, so a is definitely null there.
### INPUT ###
fn f(a: str?):
    if a != null or len(a) > 3:
        n = 0
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  a @ 2:8 -> str?
  len @ 2:21 -> dynamic
  a @ 2:25 -> null
  n @ 3:9 -> <no-type>

# Symbol types
  a (param): str?
  f (fn): fn(str?) -> void
  n (local): int

# Diagnostics
  [error] RAD30001 @ 2:25 - Argument type 'null' is not assignable to expected type 'str|list|map'

### TITLE ###
AndRightOperandInUnreachableBranch
### DESCRIPTION ###
New consequence of narrowing the right operand: with x already int,
`type_of(x) != "int"` being true is impossible, so x narrows to Never
and the operand check goes quiet. Correct - the branch can't run -
but it means an `and` can now suppress a diagnostic it used to emit.
### INPUT ###
fn f(x: int):
    if type_of(x) != "int" and x + 1 > 0:
        n = 0
### CHECK ###
# Identifier types
  type_of @ 2:8 -> dynamic
  x @ 2:16 -> int
  x @ 2:32 -> never
  n @ 3:9 -> <no-type>

# Symbol types
  f (fn): fn(int) -> void
  n (local): int
  x (param): int

# Diagnostics
  (none)
